SUMMARY:Civic Meetup #568: Hack the Count: Turning Window Sensors into Str
 eet Safety Evidence
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261110T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261110T210000
UID:6822bb3376454640b232242598ed9522@guild.host
DESCRIPTION:Topic: Hack the Count: Turning Window Sensors into Street Safe
 ty Evidence\n\nWhen Ontario removed roughly 700 automated speed enforcemen
 t cameras from school and community safety zones in 2025\, neighbourhoods 
 lost one of their few sources of hard evidence about speeding. What happen
 s when residents start collecting that evidence themselves?\n\nThis talk i
 ntroduces CIVIC (Community-centred Infrastructures for Visible and Inclusi
 ve Cities)\, an urban living lab in London\, Ontario. Residents host low-c
 ost\, privacy-by-design Telraam traffic sensors and noise monitors in thei
 r own windows. These sensors count cars\, cyclists and pedestrians and est
 imate speeds\, all without storing images. Professor Carrie Mitchell will 
 walk through how the network was built\, what the data shows\, and how res
 idents and city staff are using it to make the case for traffic calming. S
 he’ll also cover what’s hard: data gaps\, privacy trade-offs\, and kee
 ping a volunteer sensor network running. Finally\, she’ll ask what it wo
 uld take to scale community-owned street data across Ontario\, and where c
 ivic technologists can help.\n\n‎\n\nSpeaker: Carrie Mitchell (in-person
 )\n\nCarrie Mitchell is an Associate Professor in the School of Planning a
 t the University of Waterloo\, where she studies urban climate resilience\
 , community-based planning\, and\, more recently\, road safety governance.
  She is the founder of CIVIC (OurCIVICFutures.ca)\, an urban living lab fu
 nded by Waterloo’s Global Futures Fund that is deploying privacy-by-desi
 gn traffic and noise sensors with residents and community partners. Before
  joining Waterloo\, she spent five years at Canada’s International Devel
 opment Research Centre (IDRC) managing urban environment and climate resea
 rch across Asia. She lives in London\, Ontario.\n\n‎\n\nAgenda:\n\n7:00-
 7:20 Welcome and Introductions\n\n7:20-7:50 Presentation and Q\\&A/discuss
 ion\n\n7:50-9:00 Breakout groups\n\n‎\n\nCode of Conduct: https://civict
 ech.ca/about-us\n\n‎\n\nCheck in with us on the Civic Tech Toronto Slack
 : https://link.civictech.ca/chat\n\n‎\n\nAbout Us:\n\nOur weekly civic m
 eetups bring together Torontonians (designers\, coders\, urban planners\, 
 government staff\, mappers\, policy-makers\, students\, communications str
 ategists\, community organizers\, and more) who share an interest in makin
 g Toronto more responsive\, prosperous\, sustainable\, and equitable throu
 gh design\, tech\, and data.\n\nCome and be part of it!\n\nFor more info: 
 https://civictech.ca\n\n\\--\n\nSuggest a Speaker:&#x20\;\n\nInterested in
  speaking at a future event\, or know someone who'd be a great speaker? We
 'd love to hear from you: https://link.civictech.ca/speaker-suggestions\n\
 nSuggest a Venue:&#x20\;\n\nHave a space that could host up to 80 civic-mi
 nded folks for an evening? Consider recommending your venue: https://link.
 civictech.ca/venue-suggestions\n
LOCATION:Online: https://link.civictech.ca/zoom
